# AP Microeconomics — flagship content.
# IDs prefixed 01- come from AP_Lexicon_Bilingual_Vocabulary_Master.xlsx; others are AP Lexicon additions.
# All definitions, examples and questions are original and pending teacher review. Not official College Board material.
@subject ap-microeconomics | AP Microeconomics | AP 微观经济学 | Micro | teal | 1
@desc How individuals, firms and governments make choices under scarcity — markets, costs, competition and market failure.
@framework Unit titles follow the public AP Microeconomics course framework (6 units). Term placement is an editorial choice pending teacher review.

@unit 1 | Basic Economic Concepts | 基本经济概念
@topic 1.1 | Scarcity and Choice | 稀缺与选择 | Explain how scarcity forces trade-offs; Identify the opportunity cost of a decision
- scarcity | 稀缺性 | The condition in which limited resources are insufficient to satisfy all wants | 资源有限而人的欲望无限，因此每个人、企业和政府都必须做出取舍。稀缺不等于“短缺”：即使商品充足，资源仍然是有限的。 | Because a city has a limited budget, it must choose between building a new park and repairing roads. | 1 | #01-001
  mis: Scarcity only applies to poor countries — every society faces scarcity because resources are always limited relative to wants.
  mis: Scarcity means the same as a shortage — a shortage is a market condition caused by a price below equilibrium.
  rel: opportunity cost; trade-off; factors of production
  vs: shortage
  real: Your time before an exam is scarce: an hour on biology is an hour not spent on economics.
- opportunity cost | 机会成本 | The value of the next-best alternative given up when a choice is made | 做出一个选择时所放弃的“次优选择”的价值。注意：只算放弃的最好那一个，而不是所有被放弃选项的总和；它包括时间、收入等隐性成本。 | A student chooses to study economics instead of working a paid shift; the forgone wages are the opportunity cost. | 1 | #01-002
  mis: Opportunity cost is the sum of all alternatives given up — it is only the single next-best alternative.
  mis: Opportunity cost only includes money — time and forgone experiences count too.
  rel: scarcity; trade-off; production possibilities curve; comparative advantage
  vs: sunk cost
  vis: ppc
  real: Taking a gap year has an opportunity cost: the year of college progress and earnings you postpone.
  ipa: /ˌɒpərˈtjuːnəti kɒst/
- trade-off | 权衡取舍 | Giving up some of one thing to get more of another | 由于稀缺，得到一样东西往往意味着放弃另一样东西。trade-off 描述“取舍”这一行为本身，而机会成本衡量被放弃的那部分价值。 | Choosing more consumer goods today involves a trade-off with capital goods that support future growth. | 1
  rel: opportunity cost; scarcity
  vs: opportunity cost
- factors of production | 生产要素 | The resources used to produce goods and services: land, labor, capital and entrepreneurship | 生产所需的四类资源：土地（自然资源）、劳动、资本（工具、机器等人造生产资料）和企业家才能。经济学中的“资本”不是指钱。 | A bakery's ovens are capital, its bakers are labor, and the owner who takes the risk provides entrepreneurship. | 1
  mis: Capital means money — in economics, capital means physical tools, machines and buildings used in production.
  rel: scarcity; capital
- economic system | 经济体制 | The way a society organizes production and distribution, from command to market economies | 社会回答“生产什么、如何生产、为谁生产”三大问题的方式。市场经济依靠价格信号，计划经济依靠政府决策，现实中多为混合经济。 | In a market economy, rising prices signal producers to allocate more resources toward a good. | 2
  rel: scarcity; incentive
@topic 1.2 | Production Possibilities and Trade | 生产可能性与贸易 | Interpret points on, inside and outside a PPC; Determine comparative advantage using opportunity cost
- production possibilities curve | 生产可能性曲线 | A graph showing the maximum combinations of two goods an economy can produce with its resources and technology | 表示在资源和技术给定时，经济能生产的两种商品的最大组合。曲线上的点有效率，曲线内的点无效率（资源闲置），曲线外的点目前无法达到。 | A point inside the PPC shows unemployment of resources; economic growth shifts the curve outward. | 2 | #01-005
  mis: A point outside the PPC is efficient — it is unattainable with current resources and technology.
  mis: Moving along the curve shifts the curve — only changes in resources or technology shift the PPC.
  rel: opportunity cost; efficiency; economic growth
  vis: ppc
  ipa: PPC
- productive efficiency | 生产效率 | Producing at the lowest possible cost, shown by any point on the PPC | 以最低成本生产，即不浪费资源。在 PPC 上，任何位于曲线上的点都满足生产效率；在完全竞争长期均衡中，价格等于平均总成本最低点。 | A firm achieves productive efficiency when it produces where average total cost is minimized. | 2
  rel: production possibilities curve; allocative efficiency
  vs: allocative efficiency
- allocative efficiency | 配置效率 | Producing the combination of goods society values most, where price equals marginal cost | 资源被分配到社会最需要的商品上，条件是 P = MC（或边际收益等于边际成本）。它关心“生产对的东西”，而不仅是“便宜地生产”。 | A perfectly competitive market is allocatively efficient because firms produce where P = MC. | 3
  rel: productive efficiency; marginal cost; deadweight loss
  vs: productive efficiency
- comparative advantage | 比较优势 | The ability to produce a good at a lower opportunity cost than another producer | 生产某种商品的机会成本比别人低。即使一方在所有商品上都有绝对优势，双方仍可以按比较优势分工并从贸易中获益。 | Country A gives up 2 cloth per wheat while Country B gives up 4, so A has the comparative advantage in wheat. | 2 | #01-006
  mis: The country that produces more of everything should not trade — gains from trade come from differences in opportunity cost, not absolute output.
  rel: opportunity cost; absolute advantage; specialization; terms of trade
  vs: absolute advantage
- absolute advantage | 绝对优势 | The ability to produce more of a good than another producer using the same resources | 用相同的资源生产更多的产品。绝对优势比较的是产量，比较优势比较的是机会成本——贸易方向由比较优势决定。 | A worker who can assemble 10 phones an hour while another assembles 6 has an absolute advantage in assembly. | 1 | #01-007
  rel: comparative advantage; specialization
  vs: comparative advantage
- specialization | 专业化分工 | Concentrating production on goods for which a producer has a comparative advantage | 每个生产者集中生产自己机会成本最低的商品，再通过交换获得其他商品，使总产量提高。 | When two countries specialize according to comparative advantage, total world output rises. | 2
  rel: comparative advantage; terms of trade
- terms of trade | 贸易条件 | The rate at which one good is exchanged for another in trade | 两种商品交换的比例。互利的贸易条件必须位于两国各自机会成本之间。 | Trade is mutually beneficial if the terms of trade lie between the two countries' opportunity costs. | 3
  rel: comparative advantage; specialization
@topic 1.3 | Marginal Analysis | 边际分析 | Apply marginal analysis to decisions; Explain utility maximization
- marginal benefit | 边际效益 | The additional benefit gained from consuming or producing one more unit | 多消费或多生产一单位所带来的额外好处。注意区分：marginal benefit 是“边际效益”，而 marginal revenue 是厂商的“边际收益（收入）”。 | A student keeps studying while the marginal benefit of another hour exceeds its marginal cost. | 2 | #01-003
  mis: Marginal benefit is the total benefit — it is only the extra benefit from the next unit.
  rel: marginal cost; marginal utility
  vs: marginal revenue
- marginal cost | 边际成本 | The additional cost of producing one more unit of output | 每多生产一单位产品所增加的总成本，等于 ΔTC/ΔQ。厂商比较 MR 与 MC 来决定产量。 | A profit-maximizing firm compares marginal revenue and marginal cost before producing another unit. | 2 | #01-004
  mis: Marginal cost is the same as average total cost — MC is the cost of the next unit, ATC is cost per unit on average.
  rel: marginal revenue; average total cost; supply
  vs: average total cost
  vis: cost-curves
  ipa: /ˈmɑːdʒɪnəl kɒst/
- marginal utility | 边际效用 | The additional satisfaction from consuming one more unit of a good | 多消费一单位商品带来的额外满足感。通常随消费量增加而递减（边际效用递减规律）。 | The third slice of pizza usually provides less marginal utility than the first. | 2
  rel: diminishing marginal utility; utility maximization
- diminishing marginal utility | 边际效用递减 | The tendency for each additional unit consumed to add less satisfaction | 连续消费同一商品时，每多一单位带来的额外满足逐渐减少，这也是需求曲线向下倾斜的原因之一。 | Diminishing marginal utility helps explain why consumers buy more only at lower prices. | 2
  rel: marginal utility; law of demand
- utility-maximizing rule | 效用最大化法则 | Consumers maximize utility when the marginal utility per dollar is equal across goods | 消费者在预算约束下，使每一元钱在各商品上带来的边际效用相等（MUa/Pa = MUb/Pb）时，总效用最大。 | If MU per dollar for books exceeds MU per dollar for snacks, the consumer should buy more books. | 3
  rel: marginal utility; budget constraint
- sunk cost | 沉没成本 | A cost that has already been incurred and cannot be recovered | 已经发生且无法收回的成本。理性决策应忽略沉没成本，只比较未来的边际收益与边际成本。 | A firm should ignore the money already spent on a failed project when deciding whether to continue it. | 2
  mis: You should keep going to justify money already spent — sunk costs should not affect forward-looking decisions.
  vs: opportunity cost

@unit 2 | Supply and Demand | 供给与需求
@topic 2.1 | Demand and Supply Basics | 需求与供给基础 | Explain the law of demand and the law of supply; Distinguish a change in quantity demanded from a change in demand
- demand | 需求 | The quantities of a good consumers are willing and able to buy at each possible price | 在每个价格下消费者“愿意且能够”购买的数量，是一整条曲线（价格与需求量的关系），而不是某一个数量。 | An increase in consumer income raises demand for normal goods, shifting the demand curve right. | 1 | #01-008
  mis: A price change shifts the demand curve — a price change causes movement along the curve (change in quantity demanded).
  rel: law of demand; quantity demanded; determinants of demand
  vs: quantity demanded
  vis: supply-demand demand-right
- quantity demanded | 需求量 | The specific amount consumers will buy at one particular price | 某一特定价格下的购买量，是需求曲线上的一个点。价格变化引起需求量变化（沿曲线移动）。 | When the price of tickets falls, quantity demanded rises — a movement along the demand curve. | 2
  rel: demand; law of demand
  vs: demand
- law of demand | 需求定律 | Other things equal, as price rises, quantity demanded falls | 其他条件不变时，价格与需求量反向变动。由收入效应、替代效应和边际效用递减解释。 | The law of demand predicts that a higher price for gasoline reduces the quantity purchased. | 1
  rel: demand; substitution effect; income effect
- supply | 供给 | The quantities producers are willing and able to sell at each possible price | 在每个价格下生产者愿意且能够出售的数量。生产成本、技术、厂商数量等变化会使供给曲线移动。 | A new technology that lowers production costs increases supply, shifting the supply curve right. | 1 | #01-009
  mis: Higher prices shift supply right — price changes move along the supply curve.
  rel: law of supply; quantity supplied
  vis: supply-demand supply-right
- normal good | 正常品 | A good for which demand increases when income increases | 收入增加时需求上升的商品。与之相对的是低档品（劣等品）。 | Restaurant meals are a normal good for most households. | 2
  rel: inferior good; income elasticity
  vs: inferior good
- inferior good | 低档品（劣等品） | A good for which demand decreases when income increases | 收入增加时需求反而下降的商品，并非质量差，而是消费者有了更好的替代品。 | As incomes rise, demand for instant noodles may fall, so they behave as an inferior good. | 2
  rel: normal good
  vs: normal good
- substitutes | 替代品 | Goods that can replace each other, so a price rise in one increases demand for the other | 一种商品价格上升，另一种商品的需求增加。交叉价格弹性为正。 | If the price of coffee rises, demand for tea increases because they are substitutes. | 2
  rel: complements; cross-price elasticity
  vs: complements
- complements | 互补品 | Goods used together, so a price rise in one decreases demand for the other | 一起使用的商品：一种价格上升，另一种需求下降。交叉价格弹性为负。 | A rise in the price of printers decreases demand for ink cartridges. | 2
  rel: substitutes
  vs: substitutes
@topic 2.2 | Elasticity | 弹性 | Calculate price elasticity using the midpoint method; Relate elasticity to total revenue
- price elasticity of demand | 需求价格弹性 | The responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price, measured as %ΔQd ÷ %ΔP | 需求量对价格变化的敏感程度。|Ed| > 1 为富有弹性，< 1 为缺乏弹性。替代品越多、时间越长、占收入比例越大，弹性越大。 | Because insulin has few substitutes, its demand is highly inelastic. | 2 | #01-013
  mis: Elasticity is the same as slope — elasticity uses percentage changes and varies along a straight-line demand curve.
  rel: total revenue test; elastic; inelastic
  vis: elasticity
- total revenue test | 总收益检验 | Using the change in total revenue after a price change to infer whether demand is elastic or inelastic | 若降价后总收益增加，说明需求富有弹性；若降价后总收益减少，说明缺乏弹性。 | When a theater cut prices and total revenue fell, demand for tickets was inelastic. | 3
  rel: price elasticity of demand
- income elasticity | 需求收入弹性 | The responsiveness of demand to a change in income | 需求对收入变化的反应程度。正值为正常品，负值为低档品。 | A negative income elasticity indicates an inferior good. | 3
  rel: normal good; inferior good
- cross-price elasticity | 交叉价格弹性 | The responsiveness of demand for one good to a change in the price of another | 一种商品的需求对另一种商品价格变化的反应。正值为替代品，负值为互补品。 | A positive cross-price elasticity between two brands of cereal shows they are substitutes. | 3
  rel: substitutes; complements
@topic 2.3 | Market Equilibrium and Surplus | 市场均衡与剩余 | Identify equilibrium price and quantity; Measure consumer and producer surplus
- equilibrium price | 均衡价格 | The price at which quantity supplied equals quantity demanded | 供给量等于需求量时的价格，此时市场“出清”，既无短缺也无过剩。 | If demand increases while supply is unchanged, the equilibrium price and quantity both rise. | 1 | #01-010
  rel: shortage; surplus; supply; demand
  vis: supply-demand
- shortage | 短缺 | A situation where quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at the current price | 当前价格低于均衡价格时，需求量大于供给量。短缺会给价格带来上涨压力。 | A binding price ceiling creates a persistent shortage. | 1
  rel: price ceiling; equilibrium price
  vs: surplus; scarcity
- surplus | 过剩 | A situation where quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded at the current price | 当前价格高于均衡价格时，供给量大于需求量，价格面临下降压力。注意与“消费者剩余”的 surplus 不同。 | A binding minimum wage can create a surplus of labor, which is unemployment. | 1
  rel: price floor; equilibrium price
  vs: shortage; consumer surplus
- consumer surplus | 消费者剩余 | The difference between what consumers are willing to pay and what they actually pay | 消费者愿意支付的最高价格与实际支付价格之差，在图上是需求曲线以下、价格线以上的面积。 | When the price falls, consumer surplus increases. | 2 | #01-014
  rel: producer surplus; total surplus; deadweight loss
  vs: producer surplus
  vis: supply-demand surplus
- producer surplus | 生产者剩余 | The difference between the price producers receive and the minimum they would accept | 生产者实际得到的价格与其愿意接受的最低价格之差，图上是价格线以下、供给曲线以上的面积。 | A higher market price increases producer surplus. | 2 | #01-015
  rel: consumer surplus; total surplus
  vs: consumer surplus
- price ceiling | 价格上限 | A legal maximum price; binding when set below equilibrium | 政府规定的最高价格。只有低于均衡价格时才“有约束力”，会造成短缺和无谓损失。 | A rent-control ceiling below equilibrium creates a shortage of apartments. | 2 | #01-011
  mis: A price ceiling is set above equilibrium to keep prices high — a binding ceiling is set below equilibrium.
  rel: shortage; deadweight loss
  vs: price floor
  vis: supply-demand ceiling
- price floor | 价格下限 | A legal minimum price; binding when set above equilibrium | 政府规定的最低价格。只有高于均衡价格时才有约束力，会造成过剩。最低工资是劳动市场中的价格下限。 | A minimum wage set above the equilibrium wage creates a surplus of labor. | 2 | #01-012
  rel: surplus; deadweight loss
  vs: price ceiling
  vis: supply-demand floor
- deadweight loss | 无谓损失 | The loss of total surplus when a market produces an inefficient quantity | 由于产量偏离有效率水平（P≠MC 等），社会总剩余的净损失，图上通常是一个三角形。 | A per-unit tax creates deadweight loss because some mutually beneficial trades no longer occur. | 3 | #01-016
  mis: Deadweight loss is the tax revenue — tax revenue is transferred to government; deadweight loss is surplus no one receives.
  rel: allocative efficiency; tax incidence; price ceiling
- tax incidence | 税负归宿 | How the burden of a tax is divided between buyers and sellers | 税收负担在买卖双方之间的分配，弹性较小的一方承担更多税负，而与法律规定由谁缴税无关。 | When demand is more inelastic than supply, buyers bear most of the tax. | 3
  rel: deadweight loss; price elasticity of demand

@unit 3 | Production, Cost, and the Perfect Competition Model | 生产、成本与完全竞争模型
@topic 3.1 | Production and Costs | 生产与成本 | Explain diminishing marginal returns; Relate marginal cost to average costs
- marginal product | 边际产量 | The additional output produced by one more unit of an input | 多投入一单位要素（通常是劳动）所增加的产量。 | Hiring a third worker raised output from 25 to 33 units, so the marginal product of labor was 8. | 2 | #01-025
  rel: diminishing marginal returns; marginal cost
- diminishing marginal returns | 边际收益递减 | As more of a variable input is added to a fixed input, marginal product eventually falls | 在固定要素（如厂房）不变时，不断增加可变要素，边际产量最终会下降。这使边际成本曲线向上倾斜。 | Adding more cooks to a small kitchen eventually adds less output per cook. | 2
  mis: Diminishing returns means output falls — total output can still rise; only the additional output per input falls.
  rel: marginal product; marginal cost; short run
  vs: diseconomies of scale
- fixed cost | 固定成本 | A cost that does not change with the quantity of output in the short run | 短期内不随产量变化的成本，如房租。即使产量为零也要支付。 | Rent on a factory is a fixed cost because it is paid whether output is 0 or 1,000. | 1
  rel: variable cost; average fixed cost
  vs: variable cost
- variable cost | 可变成本 | A cost that changes as the quantity of output changes | 随产量变化的成本，如原材料和计时工资。 | Raw materials are a variable cost that rises with output. | 1
  vs: fixed cost
- average total cost | 平均总成本 | Total cost divided by the quantity of output | 平均每单位产品的成本 ATC = TC/Q。MC 曲线穿过 ATC 的最低点。 | When marginal cost is below average total cost, average total cost is falling. | 2 | #01-024
  mis: ATC rises whenever MC rises — ATC rises only when MC is above ATC.
  rel: marginal cost; fixed cost; break-even
  vs: marginal cost
  vis: cost-curves
- economies of scale | 规模经济 | Falling long-run average total cost as a firm increases its scale of production | 长期中扩大生产规模使平均成本下降，来源于分工、批量采购等。 | Large car manufacturers enjoy economies of scale that lower cost per vehicle. | 2
  rel: long-run average total cost
  vs: diminishing marginal returns
@topic 3.2 | Profit and Perfect Competition | 利润与完全竞争 | Apply the MR = MC rule; Explain long-run equilibrium in perfect competition
- marginal revenue | 边际收益（收入） | The additional revenue from selling one more unit of output | 多卖一单位产品增加的总收入 ΔTR/ΔQ。完全竞争厂商 MR = P；垄断厂商 MR < P。 | A profit-maximizing firm produces where marginal revenue equals marginal cost. | 2 | #01-019
  rel: marginal cost; profit maximization
  vs: marginal benefit
  vis: mr-mc
- profit maximization | 利润最大化 | Producing the quantity where marginal revenue equals marginal cost | 厂商在 MR = MC 的产量处获得最大利润（或最小亏损）。 | If MR > MC at the current output, the firm should increase production. | 2
  rel: marginal revenue; marginal cost
- economic profit | 经济利润 | Total revenue minus both explicit and implicit costs | 总收益减去显性成本和隐性成本（包括机会成本）。经济利润为零表示“正常利润”。 | Zero economic profit means the owner earns exactly what they could earn in their next-best use. | 3
  rel: accounting profit; opportunity cost; normal profit
  vs: accounting profit
- accounting profit | 会计利润 | Total revenue minus explicit costs only | 只扣除显性成本（实际支付的费用）的利润，因此通常高于经济利润。 | A shop can show an accounting profit while earning negative economic profit. | 3
  vs: economic profit
- perfect competition | 完全竞争 | A market structure with many firms selling identical products, free entry and exit, and price-taking firms | 大量厂商出售同质产品、自由进出，每个厂商都是价格接受者。长期中经济利润为零，且同时实现配置效率和生产效率。 | In perfect competition, short-run profits attract entry until economic profit falls to zero. | 2 | #01-020
  rel: price taker; long-run equilibrium; allocative efficiency
  vs: monopolistic competition
- price taker | 价格接受者 | A firm that cannot affect market price and must accept it | 厂商太小，无法影响市场价格，只能按市场价格出售，面对的需求曲线是水平的。 | A single wheat farmer is a price taker facing a perfectly elastic demand curve. | 2
  rel: perfect competition
- shutdown point | 停业点 | The price equal to minimum average variable cost, below which a firm shuts down in the short run | 短期中，如果价格低于平均可变成本最低点，厂商停产可以使亏损最小（只损失固定成本）。 | If price falls below AVC, the firm minimizes losses by shutting down temporarily. | 3
  rel: average variable cost; break-even point
  vs: break-even point
- break-even point | 盈亏平衡点 | The price equal to minimum average total cost, where economic profit is zero | 价格等于 ATC 最低点，经济利润为零。 | At the break-even point the firm covers all costs, including opportunity costs. | 3
  vs: shutdown point

@unit 4 | Imperfect Competition | 不完全竞争
@topic 4.1 | Monopoly | 垄断 | Explain why MR lies below demand for a monopolist; Compare monopoly and competitive outcomes
- monopoly | 垄断 | A market with a single seller of a product with no close substitutes and high barriers to entry | 只有一个卖方、没有相近替代品且进入壁垒很高的市场。垄断者在 MR = MC 处生产，并根据需求曲线定价，导致产量更低、价格更高和无谓损失。 | A monopolist charges a price above marginal cost, creating deadweight loss. | 2 | #01-021
  mis: A monopolist can charge any price it wants — it is still limited by the demand curve.
  rel: barriers to entry; price maker; natural monopoly
  vs: oligopoly
  vis: monopoly
- barriers to entry | 进入壁垒 | Obstacles that prevent new firms from entering a market | 阻止新厂商进入的因素，如专利、规模经济、政府许可或对关键资源的控制。 | Patents act as a barrier to entry in pharmaceutical markets. | 2
  rel: monopoly; oligopoly
- natural monopoly | 自然垄断 | A market where one firm can supply the whole market at lower cost than multiple firms due to economies of scale | 由于规模经济巨大，一家企业供应整个市场的成本最低，如自来水、电网。 | Electric utilities are often natural monopolies regulated by the government. | 3
  rel: economies of scale; monopoly
- price discrimination | 价格歧视 | Charging different prices to different consumers for the same good when cost differences do not justify it | 对同一商品向不同消费者收取不同价格。需要市场力量、能区分消费者、防止转售。完全价格歧视会消除消费者剩余。 | Airlines practice price discrimination by charging business travelers more than tourists. | 3
  rel: monopoly; consumer surplus
@topic 4.2 | Oligopoly and Monopolistic Competition | 寡头与垄断竞争 | Use a payoff matrix to find a Nash equilibrium; Explain excess capacity
- oligopoly | 寡头垄断 | A market dominated by a few large, interdependent firms | 少数大企业主导市场，彼此决策相互依赖，常用博弈论分析。 | Because airlines are interdependent, one carrier's fare cut prompts rivals to respond. | 2 | #01-022
  rel: game theory; Nash equilibrium; collusion
  vs: monopoly; monopolistic competition
- monopolistic competition | 垄断竞争 | A market with many firms selling differentiated products and easy entry | 大量厂商出售有差异的产品，进入容易。短期可获利，长期经济利润为零，但存在过剩产能。 | Restaurants compete through differentiation, a feature of monopolistic competition. | 2 | #01-023
  rel: product differentiation; excess capacity
  vs: perfect competition; oligopoly
- game theory | 博弈论 | The study of strategic decision-making when outcomes depend on others' choices | 研究参与者在结果相互影响时如何做策略选择的理论，用支付矩阵（payoff matrix）表示。 | A payoff matrix shows how each firm's profit depends on its rival's pricing strategy. | 3
  rel: dominant strategy; Nash equilibrium
- dominant strategy | 占优策略 | A strategy that gives a player a better payoff no matter what the other player does | 无论对手如何选择，该策略都给自己带来更高收益。 | Advertising is a dominant strategy if it yields higher profit whether or not the rival advertises. | 3
  rel: Nash equilibrium; game theory
  vs: Nash equilibrium
- Nash equilibrium | 纳什均衡 | An outcome where no player can gain by changing strategy, given the other's strategy | 在对方策略不变时，任何一方都不能通过单方面改变策略获得更高收益的状态。 | Both firms choosing low prices can be a Nash equilibrium even if both would earn more by colluding. | 3
  vs: dominant strategy
- collusion | 合谋 | An agreement among firms to limit competition, such as by fixing prices | 企业之间约定限制竞争（如共同定价、划分市场），在许多国家违法，且由于有作弊动机而难以维持。 | A cartel is a formal form of collusion. | 3
  rel: oligopoly; cartel

@unit 5 | Factor Markets | 要素市场
@topic 5.1 | Labor and Factor Demand | 劳动与要素需求 | Explain derived demand; Apply the MRP = MRC hiring rule
- derived demand | 派生需求 | Demand for a factor of production that comes from demand for the goods it helps produce | 对生产要素的需求源于对其生产的最终产品的需求。 | When demand for houses rises, demand for carpenters rises because labor demand is derived demand. | 2
  rel: marginal revenue product
- marginal revenue product | 边际收益产品 | The additional revenue generated by hiring one more unit of a factor | 多雇用一单位要素所增加的总收入 MRP = MP × MR。它构成了厂商对要素的需求曲线。 | A firm hires workers until marginal revenue product equals marginal resource cost. | 3
  rel: marginal resource cost; marginal product
  vs: marginal revenue
- marginal resource cost | 边际要素成本 | The additional cost of hiring one more unit of a factor | 多雇一单位要素增加的总成本。在完全竞争劳动市场中等于工资。 | In a monopsony, marginal resource cost exceeds the wage. | 3
  rel: marginal revenue product; monopsony
- monopsony | 买方垄断 | A market with a single buyer of a factor, such as one employer in a town | 某要素只有一个买方（如小镇上唯一的雇主），它雇用的工人更少、支付的工资更低。 | A monopsony employer hires fewer workers and pays a lower wage than a competitive labor market. | 3
  rel: marginal resource cost
  vs: monopoly
- least-cost rule | 最小成本法则 | Firms minimize cost when the marginal product per dollar is equal across all inputs | 厂商使每一元花在各种要素上的边际产量相等（MPL/w = MPK/r）时，成本最低。 | If MP per dollar is higher for capital than for labor, the firm should use more capital. | 3
  rel: marginal product

@unit 6 | Market Failure and the Role of Government | 市场失灵与政府的作用
@topic 6.1 | Externalities and Public Goods | 外部性与公共物品 | Graph negative and positive externalities; Explain why public goods are underprovided
- externality | 外部性 | A cost or benefit of an activity that falls on a third party not involved in the transaction | 交易对未参与交易的第三方造成的成本或收益。负外部性导致过度生产，正外部性导致生产不足。 | Pollution from a factory imposes a negative externality on nearby residents. | 2 | #01-017
  mis: Externalities always mean pollution — positive externalities like vaccination are also externalities.
  rel: marginal social cost; Pigouvian tax; market failure
  vis: externality
- marginal social cost | 边际社会成本 | Marginal private cost plus any external cost | 边际私人成本加上外部成本。存在负外部性时 MSC > MPC，社会最优产量低于市场产量。 | With a negative externality, the socially optimal quantity is where MSB equals MSC. | 3
  rel: externality; marginal social benefit
- marginal social benefit | 边际社会收益 | Marginal private benefit plus any external benefit | 边际私人收益加外部收益。存在正外部性时 MSB > MPB，市场产量不足。 | Because education creates external benefits, MSB exceeds MPB and the market underprovides it. | 3
  rel: externality; marginal social cost
- Pigouvian tax | 庇古税 | A per-unit tax equal to the external cost, used to correct a negative externality | 对产生负外部性的活动征收的税，使私人成本等于社会成本，从而达到社会最优产量。 | A carbon tax is a Pigouvian tax intended to reduce emissions to the socially optimal level. | 3
  rel: externality; marginal social cost
- public good | 公共物品 | A good that is nonrival and nonexcludable | 具有非竞争性（一个人使用不减少他人使用）和非排他性（无法阻止不付费者使用）的物品，因此存在“搭便车”问题。 | National defense is a public good because no one can be excluded from its protection. | 2 | #01-018
  mis: Any good the government provides is a public good — public schools are excludable, so they are not pure public goods.
  rel: free-rider problem; nonrival; nonexcludable
- free-rider problem | 搭便车问题 | When people benefit from a good without paying, causing it to be underprovided by markets | 人们可以不付费就享受物品，因此私人市场缺乏提供动力，导致供给不足。 | Fireworks displays face a free-rider problem because people can watch without paying. | 2
  rel: public good
- market failure | 市场失灵 | A situation where a free market fails to allocate resources efficiently | 市场无法实现资源有效配置，原因包括外部性、公共物品、市场势力和信息不对称。 | Externalities are a source of market failure that may justify government intervention. | 2
  rel: externality; public good; deadweight loss
@topic 6.2 | Inequality and Government Policy | 不平等与政府政策 | Interpret a Lorenz curve; Evaluate the efficiency effects of taxes and subsidies
- Lorenz curve | 洛伦兹曲线 | A graph showing the cumulative share of income received by cumulative shares of the population | 表示人口累计比例与收入累计比例关系的曲线，越偏离45度线，收入越不平等。 | A Lorenz curve that bows farther from the line of equality shows greater inequality. | 3
  rel: Gini coefficient
- Gini coefficient | 基尼系数 | A number from 0 to 1 summarizing income inequality, based on the Lorenz curve | 衡量收入不平等的指标，0 表示完全平等，1 表示完全不平等。 | A rise in the Gini coefficient indicates that income inequality increased. | 3
  rel: Lorenz curve
- progressive tax | 累进税 | A tax that takes a larger percentage of income as income rises | 收入越高，税率越高的税制。 | A progressive income tax reduces after-tax income inequality. | 2
  rel: regressive tax
  vs: regressive tax
- regressive tax | 累退税 | A tax that takes a smaller percentage of income as income rises | 收入越高，税负占收入的比例越低，例如对生活必需品征收的销售税。 | A sales tax on necessities is regressive because lower-income households spend a larger share of income. | 2
  vs: progressive tax
- per-unit subsidy | 单位补贴 | A government payment to producers or consumers for each unit of a good | 政府对每单位商品提供的补贴，可用来纠正正外部性，使产量接近社会最优。 | A per-unit subsidy for vaccines can move output toward the socially optimal quantity. | 2
  rel: externality; marginal social benefit

# ---------------- Practice items (original; not official College Board questions) ----------------
@q micro-u1-001 | UNDERSTAND | CONCEPT | SCENARIO | 1 | 1 | opportunity cost
? Maya can either work a paid shift that earns $60 or attend an economics review session. She attends the review session. Which concept best describes the $60?
=* Opportunity cost :: Correct — the $60 is the value of the next-best alternative Maya gave up.
= Sunk cost :: A sunk cost is already spent and unrecoverable; the $60 is a forgone future earning.
= Marginal benefit :: Marginal benefit is the extra benefit of the review session, not what she gave up.
= Scarcity :: Scarcity explains why she must choose, but the $60 itself measures the opportunity cost.
! The question asks you to name what was given up. Opportunity cost is the value of the next-best alternative forgone — here, the $60 of wages.
!zh 题目问的是“被放弃的东西”。机会成本 = 被放弃的次优选择的价值，即 60 美元工资。
@q micro-u1-002 | APPLY | APPLICATION | GRAPH | 1 | 2 | production possibilities curve; productive efficiency
? On a production possibilities curve, an economy is producing at a point inside the curve. Which statement is accurate?
vis: ppc
= The economy is allocatively efficient but productively inefficient. :: A point inside the curve is not productively efficient, and allocative efficiency cannot be judged this way.
=* Some resources are unemployed or used inefficiently. :: Correct — interior points mean output could rise for both goods without any trade-off.
= The economy has experienced economic growth. :: Growth shifts the PPC outward; it does not place the economy inside the curve.
= The combination is unattainable with current resources. :: Points outside the curve are unattainable; inside points are attainable.
! Points inside the PPC show underused resources (for example, unemployment). Output of both goods could increase without giving anything up.
!zh PPC 内部的点表示资源未被充分利用（如失业），可以在不放弃任何商品的情况下同时增加两种商品的产量。
@q micro-u1-003 | APPLY | APPLICATION | MCQ | 1 | 2 | comparative advantage; opportunity cost
? In one hour, Country X can produce 10 cars or 20 computers; Country Y can produce 4 cars or 12 computers. Which country has the comparative advantage in cars?
=* Country X, because its opportunity cost of a car is 2 computers versus 3 for Country Y. :: Correct — compare opportunity costs: X gives up 2 computers per car, Y gives up 3.
= Country X, because it can produce more cars. :: That describes absolute advantage, not comparative advantage.
= Country Y, because it has a lower opportunity cost of cars. :: Y's opportunity cost of a car is 3 computers, which is higher than X's 2.
= Neither, because Country X has an absolute advantage in both goods. :: Absolute advantage in both goods does not eliminate comparative advantage.
! Comparative advantage depends on opportunity cost. X: 1 car = 20/10 = 2 computers. Y: 1 car = 12/4 = 3 computers. X's cost is lower.
!zh 比较优势看机会成本：X 生产 1 辆车放弃 2 台电脑，Y 放弃 3 台，所以 X 在汽车上有比较优势。
@q micro-u1-004 | MASTER | APPLICATION | WRITTEN | 1 | 3 | marginal benefit; marginal cost
? Explain why a rational decision-maker continues an activity only while marginal benefit is greater than or equal to marginal cost. Use a specific example.
r: States that the decision is made at the margin (one more unit at a time).
r: Explains that net benefit rises when MB > MC and falls when MB < MC.
r: Identifies the optimal stopping point where MB = MC.
r: Gives a specific, accurate example (e.g., hours of study, units produced).
! A strong answer compares the extra benefit and extra cost of the next unit: while MB ≥ MC, doing one more unit adds to (or does not reduce) total net benefit; beyond MB = MC, each extra unit lowers net benefit.
!zh 关键在“边际”：只要下一单位的边际效益不小于边际成本，继续做会增加净收益；超过 MB = MC 后，每多做一单位都会减少净收益。
@q micro-u2-001 | UNDERSTAND | CONCEPT | SCENARIO | 2 | 1 | demand; quantity demanded
? The price of concert tickets falls and fans buy more tickets. An economist would describe this as
= an increase in demand. :: A change in the good's own price does not shift the demand curve.
=* an increase in quantity demanded. :: Correct — a lower price causes a movement down along the existing demand curve.
= a decrease in supply. :: Nothing in the scenario changes sellers' costs or willingness to sell.
= a shortage. :: A shortage requires price to be held below equilibrium.
! Own-price changes cause movement along the demand curve (change in quantity demanded). Only non-price determinants (income, tastes, prices of related goods, expectations, number of buyers) shift demand.
!zh 自身价格变化 → 沿需求曲线移动（需求量变化）；收入、偏好、相关商品价格等非价格因素变化 → 需求曲线移动（需求变化）。
@q micro-u2-002 | APPLY | APPLICATION | GRAPH | 2 | 2 | price ceiling; shortage
? The government sets a maximum rent of $900 in a market where the equilibrium rent is $1,200. What is the most likely result?
vis: supply-demand ceiling
= A surplus of apartments, because landlords supply more at a lower price. :: At a lower price quantity supplied falls, not rises.
=* A shortage of apartments, because quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied. :: Correct — a binding ceiling below equilibrium creates excess demand.
= No effect, because the ceiling is below equilibrium. :: A ceiling below equilibrium is binding; one above equilibrium has no effect.
= An increase in producer surplus. :: Landlords receive a lower price, so producer surplus falls.
! A price ceiling is binding only when set below the equilibrium price. At $900, Qd > Qs, so a shortage results and deadweight loss appears.
!zh 价格上限只有低于均衡价格时才有约束力。900 美元时需求量 > 供给量，出现短缺并产生无谓损失。
@q micro-u2-003 | APPLY | APPLICATION | MCQ | 2 | 2 | price elasticity of demand; total revenue test
? A coffee shop raises its price by 10% and its total revenue rises. What can be concluded about demand for its coffee over this price range?
= Demand is elastic. :: With elastic demand, a price increase lowers total revenue.
=* Demand is inelastic. :: Correct — revenue rose, so the percentage drop in quantity was smaller than the 10% price rise.
= Demand is unit elastic. :: Unit elastic demand would leave total revenue unchanged.
= Coffee is an inferior good. :: Income elasticity, not price elasticity, determines whether a good is inferior.
! Total revenue test: if price and total revenue move in the same direction, demand is inelastic.
!zh 总收益检验：价格与总收益同方向变动 → 需求缺乏弹性。
@q micro-u2-004 | UNDERSTAND | CONCEPT | SCENARIO | 2 | 2 | inferior good; normal good
? After a raise, Leo buys fewer bus tickets and more rides from a ride-share app. For Leo, bus tickets are
= a normal good. :: Demand for a normal good rises when income rises.
=* an inferior good. :: Correct — his demand for bus tickets fell when his income rose.
= a complement to ride-shares. :: Complements are used together; Leo is replacing one with the other.
= a public good. :: Bus tickets are excludable, so they are not a public good.
! When income rises and demand falls, the good is inferior for that consumer. "Inferior" describes the income relationship, not quality.
!zh 收入上升而需求下降 → 低档品。“低档”描述的是需求与收入的关系，并非质量差。
@q micro-u2-005 | APPLY | CONCEPT | CLOZE | 2 | 2 | deadweight loss
? A per-unit tax reduces the quantity traded below the efficient level. The lost total surplus that no one receives is called ______.
a: deadweight loss|deadweight-loss|dwl
! Tax revenue is a transfer to the government; deadweight loss is the surplus lost because some mutually beneficial trades no longer happen.
!zh 税收收入是转移给政府的部分；无谓损失是因交易减少而消失、没有任何人得到的剩余。
@q micro-u3-001 | APPLY | APPLICATION | GRAPH | 3 | 2 | marginal cost; average total cost
? A firm's marginal cost is currently below its average total cost. If the firm produces one more unit, average total cost will
vis: cost-curves
=* decrease. :: Correct — when the marginal unit costs less than the average, the average falls.
= increase. :: ATC rises only when MC is above ATC.
= stay the same. :: ATC is unchanged only where MC = ATC (the minimum of ATC).
= equal marginal revenue. :: Nothing requires ATC to equal MR.
! The marginal-average rule: if MC < ATC, ATC falls; if MC > ATC, ATC rises. MC crosses ATC at its minimum.
!zh 边际-平均规律：MC < ATC 时 ATC 下降；MC > ATC 时 ATC 上升；MC 穿过 ATC 的最低点。
@q micro-u3-002 | MASTER | APPLICATION | MCQ | 3 | 3 | perfect competition; economic profit
? Perfectly competitive firms in an industry are earning positive economic profits. What will happen in the long run?
= Firms will exit, decreasing market supply. :: Firms exit when there are losses, not profits.
=* Firms will enter, increasing market supply until economic profit is zero. :: Correct — free entry pushes price down to minimum ATC.
= The market price will rise as firms collude. :: Perfectly competitive firms cannot collude or set price.
= Each firm will raise its price above marginal cost. :: Price takers cannot raise price above the market price.
! Positive economic profit signals entry. Market supply increases, price falls, and long-run equilibrium occurs at P = MC = minimum ATC.
!zh 正的经济利润吸引新厂商进入，市场供给增加，价格下降，直到 P = MC = 最低 ATC，经济利润为零。
@q micro-u3-003 | UNDERSTAND | CONCEPT | SCENARIO | 3 | 3 | shutdown point; break-even point
? A firm's price is below its average total cost but above its average variable cost. In the short run, the firm should
= shut down immediately to avoid all losses. :: Shutting down still leaves the firm paying fixed costs, which would be a larger loss.
=* continue producing, because revenue covers variable costs and part of fixed costs. :: Correct — operating minimizes losses when P > AVC.
= raise its price to cover average total cost. :: A price-taking firm cannot raise its price.
= exit the industry immediately. :: Exit is a long-run decision.
! Short-run rule: operate if P ≥ AVC (shutdown point is minimum AVC). The firm loses money but less than its fixed costs.
!zh 短期规则：只要 P ≥ AVC 就继续生产，此时亏损小于固定成本；P < AVC 才停业。
@q micro-u4-001 | APPLY | APPLICATION | GRAPH | 4 | 2 | monopoly; deadweight loss
? Compared with a perfectly competitive industry with the same costs, an unregulated profit-maximizing monopoly will
vis: monopoly
=* produce less output at a higher price, creating deadweight loss. :: Correct — the monopolist restricts output to where MR = MC and charges a price above MC.
= produce more output at a lower price due to economies of scale. :: Only true for some natural monopolies; the general model predicts lower output.
= produce where price equals marginal cost. :: P = MC is the competitive outcome; monopoly sets P > MC.
= earn zero economic profit in the long run. :: Barriers to entry let a monopoly keep profits in the long run.
! A monopolist sets quantity where MR = MC, then charges the price on the demand curve. Since P > MC, output is below the allocatively efficient level.
!zh 垄断者在 MR = MC 处决定产量，再按需求曲线定价，P > MC，产量低于有效率水平，产生无谓损失。
@q micro-u4-002 | APPLY | APPLICATION | PASSAGE | 4 | 3 | dominant strategy; Nash equilibrium
> Two firms, Alpha and Beta, each choose a High or Low price. Payoffs (Alpha, Beta) in thousands: High/High (50, 50); High/Low (20, 70); Low/High (70, 20); Low/Low (30, 30).
? Which statement is correct?
= Alpha has no dominant strategy. :: Low gives Alpha more whether Beta chooses High (70 > 50) or Low (30 > 20).
=* Both firms have a dominant strategy of Low, so Low/Low is the Nash equilibrium. :: Correct — each firm earns more with Low regardless of the rival's choice.
= High/High is the Nash equilibrium because joint profit is highest. :: Each firm could gain by switching to Low, so High/High is unstable.
= The game has no Nash equilibrium. :: Low/Low is a Nash equilibrium.
! Check each player: if Beta picks High, Alpha prefers Low (70 > 50); if Beta picks Low, Alpha prefers Low (30 > 20). By symmetry Beta also prefers Low.
!zh 逐个检查：无论 Beta 选什么，Alpha 选 Low 收益都更高，反之亦然，所以 Low/Low 是纳什均衡（也是囚徒困境）。
@q micro-u5-001 | UNDERSTAND | CONCEPT | SCENARIO | 5 | 2 | derived demand
? A surge in demand for electric vehicles increases the demand for battery engineers. This relationship is an example of
=* derived demand. :: Correct — demand for engineers comes from demand for the cars they help build.
= diminishing marginal returns. :: That describes falling marginal product, not why factor demand rises.
= monopsony. :: Monopsony refers to a single buyer of labor.
= comparative advantage. :: Comparative advantage concerns opportunity cost in production.
! Demand for factors is derived from demand for the final product.
!zh 要素需求来源于对最终产品的需求，即派生需求。
@q micro-u6-001 | APPLY | APPLICATION | GRAPH | 6 | 2 | externality; marginal social cost; Pigouvian tax
? A factory's production creates pollution. Without government action, the market quantity is
vis: externality
=* greater than the socially optimal quantity, because marginal social cost exceeds marginal private cost. :: Correct — producers ignore the external cost and overproduce.
= less than the socially optimal quantity, because the factory underestimates demand. :: Negative externalities lead to overproduction, not underproduction.
= equal to the socially optimal quantity, because markets are efficient. :: Externalities are a source of market failure.
= zero, because pollution is illegal. :: The scenario does not involve a ban.
! With a negative externality, MSC > MPC. The market produces where MPC = MPB, which is more than the optimal quantity where MSC = MSB. A Pigouvian tax equal to the external cost can correct this.
!zh 负外部性：MSC > MPC，市场产量（MPC = MPB）大于社会最优产量（MSC = MSB）。征收等于外部成本的庇古税可以纠正。
@q micro-u6-002 | UNDERSTAND | CONCEPT | SCENARIO | 6 | 2 | public good; free-rider problem
? A town's lighthouse benefits every passing ship, and it is impossible to stop a ship that has not paid from seeing the light. The lighthouse is best described as
= a private good. :: Private goods are rival and excludable.
=* a public good, which tends to be underprovided because of free riders. :: Correct — it is nonrival and nonexcludable.
= a common resource. :: Common resources are rival; one ship seeing the light does not reduce it for others.
= a natural monopoly. :: The issue is non-excludability, not cost structure.
! Public goods are nonrival and nonexcludable, so people can benefit without paying — the free-rider problem.
!zh 公共物品具有非竞争性和非排他性，人们可以不付费就受益，因此存在搭便车问题。

# ---------------- Confusion Lab pairs ----------------
@pair mc-vs-atc | marginal cost | average total cost
! Marginal cost is the cost of the NEXT unit; average total cost is the cost PER unit across all units produced.
!zh 边际成本 = 下一单位的成本；平均总成本 = 所有产量平均每单位的成本。
d: Formula :: ΔTC ÷ ΔQ :: TC ÷ Q
d: Used for :: Deciding whether to produce one more unit (MR = MC) :: Judging profit per unit (P vs ATC)
d: Graph shape :: Falls then rises; crosses ATC at its minimum :: U-shaped
s: A :: A bakery's cost rises from $200 to $203 when it bakes one more cake. :: The $3 is the cost of one additional unit.
s: B :: A bakery spends $2,000 to bake 400 cakes, or $5 per cake. :: Total cost divided by quantity is average total cost.
s: A :: A firm compares the extra cost of one more unit with the price it receives. :: The MR = MC rule uses marginal cost.
s: B :: A firm earns a profit because the price is above its cost per unit. :: Profit per unit compares price with ATC.
vis: cost-curves
@pair comparative-vs-absolute | comparative advantage | absolute advantage
! Absolute advantage compares output; comparative advantage compares opportunity cost. Trade is based on comparative advantage.
!zh 绝对优势比较产量；比较优势比较机会成本。贸易方向由比较优势决定。
d: Compares :: Opportunity cost :: Quantity of output
d: Can a producer have it in both goods? :: No (with two goods and different costs) :: Yes
d: Determines trade? :: Yes :: No
s: A :: Brazil gives up fewer cars per ton of coffee than Germany. :: Lower opportunity cost = comparative advantage.
s: B :: A chef can chop more vegetables per hour than her assistant. :: More output with the same resources = absolute advantage.
s: A :: Even though one worker is faster at everything, both gain by specializing. :: Gains from trade come from comparative advantage.
s: B :: Country X produces 50 tons of wheat per worker; Country Y produces 30. :: Output comparison = absolute advantage.
@pair demand-vs-qd | demand | quantity demanded
! A change in the good's own price changes quantity demanded (movement along the curve); any other determinant changes demand (shift of the curve).
!zh 自身价格变化 → 需求量变化（沿曲线移动）；其他因素变化 → 需求变化（曲线移动）。
d: Caused by :: Income, tastes, related prices, expectations, number of buyers :: The good's own price
d: On the graph :: The whole curve shifts :: Movement along the curve
s: A :: A celebrity endorsement makes a sneaker more popular at every price. :: Tastes changed, so demand shifts.
s: B :: A store discounts sneakers and sells more pairs. :: Own-price change = change in quantity demanded.
s: A :: Consumers expect phone prices to rise next month and buy more now. :: Expectations shift demand.
s: B :: A higher price for movie tickets leads to fewer tickets purchased. :: Movement along the demand curve.
vis: supply-demand demand-right
@pair ceiling-vs-floor | price ceiling | price floor
! A binding price ceiling is set BELOW equilibrium and causes shortages; a binding price floor is set ABOVE equilibrium and causes surpluses.
!zh 有约束力的价格上限低于均衡价格，造成短缺；有约束力的价格下限高于均衡价格，造成过剩。
d: Legal limit :: Maximum price :: Minimum price
d: Binding when :: Below equilibrium :: Above equilibrium
d: Result :: Shortage :: Surplus
s: A :: A city caps rents for apartments below the market rate. :: A maximum price = ceiling.
s: B :: The government guarantees farmers at least $5 per bushel, above the market price. :: A minimum price = floor.
s: B :: A minimum wage above equilibrium leads to more job seekers than jobs. :: A floor in the labor market creates a surplus of labor.
s: A :: During an emergency, a law bans selling bottled water above $1. :: A legal maximum = ceiling.
